  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Holding $t$ Fixed During Convolution",
    "lesson_core_move": "Treat t as a fixed output-time parameter while letting $\\tau$ vary to set up the convolution integrand $x(\\tau)$$h(t-\\tau)$ for a continuous-time LTI system.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Holding $t$ Fixed During Convolution",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Holding $t$ Fixed During Convolution",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the convolution integral, $t$ and $\\tau$ do different jobs. The symbol $t$ names the output time, and $\\tau$ is the integration variable that scans through input time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To find one output value, choose one value of $t$ and hold it fixed. Then let $\\tau$ run through the integral. During that one integral, $t$ is not another variable of integration; it is the chosen output time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t_0)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t_0-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "After fixing $t=t_0$, the input factor stays $x(\\tau)$, and the impulse-response factor becomes $h(t_0-\\tau)$. Both factors are now read as functions of $\\tau$ for that chosen output time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{integrand at }t=t_0:\\quad x(\\tau)h(t_0-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The result is one $\\tau$-dependent integrand. Integrating it over $\\tau$ produces the single output value $y(t_0)$. Choosing another output time means holding a new value of $t$ fixed and forming a new integrand.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Holding $t$ Fixed During Convolution",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Substituting a Fixed Output Time into $h(t-\\tau)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $h(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$, rewrite $h(t-\\tau)$ when the output time is fixed at $t=3$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the fixed output time $t=3$. In $h(t-\\tau)$, substitute $3$ only for $t$ and leave $\\tau$ as the variable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t-\\tau \\quad \\longrightarrow \\quad 3-\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the shifted impulse-response factor becomes $h(3-\\tau)$. Now use the formula for $h$: whatever argument is inside $h(\\cdot)$ replaces the argument in $e^{-(\\cdot)}u(\\cdot)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h(3-\\tau)=e^{-(3-\\tau)}u(3-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Thus, for the fixed output time $t=3$, $h(t-\\tau)$ is rewritten as $e^{-(3-\\tau)}u(3-\\tau)$. The expression still depends on $\\tau$, so it is ready to be used as a $\\tau$-varying factor in the convolution setup.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Holding $t$ Fixed During Convolution",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Forming $x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)$ with $t$ Fixed",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(\\tau)=\\tau u(\\tau)$ and $h(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$, form $x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)$ for fixed output time $t=2$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the fixed output time $t=2$. First rewrite the impulse-response factor by substituting $2$ only for $t$ in $h(t-\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h(t-\\tau)\\big|_{t=2}=h(2-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now use the formula $h(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$. The argument of $h$ is $2-\\tau$, so replace the argument in the exponential and in the unit step with $2-\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h(2-\\tau)=e^{-(2-\\tau)}u(2-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The input factor is already written as a function of $\\tau$, so it stays $x(\\tau)=\\tau u(\\tau)$. Form the integrand by multiplying this factor by the rewritten impulse-response factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)h(2-\\tau)=\\tau u(\\tau)e^{-(2-\\tau)}u(2-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The final integrand contains $\\tau$ but no free $t$. That means $t$ has been fixed at $2$, while $\\tau$ remains the variable for the convolution setup.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Holding $t$ Fixed During Convolution",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Comparing the Integrand for Different Fixed Values of $t$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(\\tau)=u(\\tau)$ and $h(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$, form and compare $x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)$ for fixed output times $t=1$ and $t=4$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that two different output times are being checked. Form one $\\tau$-dependent integrand for $t=1$, then form a separate $\\tau$-dependent integrand for $t=4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $t=1$, substitute $1$ into the impulse-response factor and keep the input factor as $x(\\tau)=u(\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nh(1-\\tau)&=e^{-(1-\\tau)}u(1-\\tau)\\\\\nx(\\tau)h(1-\\tau)&=e^{-(1-\\tau)}u(\\tau)u(1-\\tau)\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $t=4$, repeat the same process with $4$ as the fixed output time. The input factor $u(\\tau)$ does not change.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nh(4-\\tau)&=e^{-(4-\\tau)}u(4-\\tau)\\\\\nx(\\tau)h(4-\\tau)&=e^{-(4-\\tau)}u(\\tau)u(4-\\tau)\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The only change is the fixed output-time value inside the impulse-response factor: $1-\\tau$ becomes $4-\\tau$. In both integrands, $\\tau$ is still the only variable left, and the input factor $u(\\tau)$ stays the same.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Holding $t$ Fixed During Convolution",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Recognizing $\\tau$ as the Only Variable Left to Integrate",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Decide whether $x(\\tau)h(3-\\tau)=\\tau e^{-(3-\\tau)}u(\\tau)u(3-\\tau)$ is ready to integrate over $\\tau$, and explain what variable remains.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that this is a readiness check, not an evaluation problem. Inspect the expression for any remaining free $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)h(3-\\tau)=\\tau e^{-(3-\\tau)}u(\\tau)u(3-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "There is no free $t$ left in the expression. The appearance of $3-\\tau$ shows that the output time has already been fixed at $t=3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{fixed output time: }t=3\\qquad \\text{remaining variable: }\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Every nonconstant part of the integrand depends on $\\tau$: $\\tau$, $u(\\tau)$, $3-\\tau$, and $u(3-\\tau)$. So $\\tau$ is the only variable left to scan during integration.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(3)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\tau e^{-(3-\\tau)}u(\\tau)u(3-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, the expression is ready to integrate over $\\tau$ to compute the one output value $y(3)$. The setup stops here; evaluating the integral is a separate step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
